<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Land of Punt</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A fabled land of incense, ebony and gold on the southern Red Sea, reached by Egyptian trading fleets for over a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Punt is known almost entirely from Egyptian sources. The earliest recorded expedition went out under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahure">Sahure</a> of the 5th Dynasty, c. 2450 BCE, and later voyages sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> harbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mersa-gawasis">Mersa Gawasis</a>. Its exact location is still debated, with proposals ranging from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> coast, while recent isotope studies of baboon mummies point to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> prized Punt for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> and frankincense, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold, leopard skins and live baboons. The best-known record, the reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut's</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a> (c. 1470 BCE), show houses on stilts, domed huts, a ruler called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parahu">Parahu</a> and his queen Ati, and myrrh trees being loaded in baskets for replanting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Nothing is known of its own language or institutions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>References fade after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, with the last expeditions recorded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> in the 12th century BCE. Punt survived in Egyptian memory as a semi-mythical 'God's Land', and some historians see it as a forerunner of the trade networks later dominated by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dmt">D'mt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/land-of-punt.jpg" alt="Land of Punt" width="320" height="369"><figcaption>Image: ArdadN at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_1450_BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Parahu, Ati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a href="/countries/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, <a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a>, <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Punt, Pwenet</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1800&amp;focus=land-of-punt" aria-label="Land of Punt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6005 1823 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Land of Punt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6325 2079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6325 2079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1800&amp;focus=land-of-punt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

